the busiest US border crossing is open  again this morning after a confrontation  forced it to close temporarily yesterday  US Customs and Border Patrol agents  fired tear gas at hundreds of people  including women and children after some  of them tried to force their way across  the border between San Diego and Tijuana  Mexico thousands of migrants are waiting  in that area still they're upset about  the slow pace of the u.s. Asylum process  Mireya Villarreal is in Tijuana outside  the San Ysidro Port of Entry mateo good  morning to you well good morning this  official port of entry the busiest in  the Western Hemisphere was shut down  yesterday for five hours after the  clashes Mexican police tried to break up  protesters and that's when some made a  run for the border that's the sound of  tear gas being fired at migrants Sunday  after they rushed the border between San  Diego and Tijuana Mexico police started  throwing tear gas at us this migrant  from hundred I said there were many  children who fainted video from a  Mexican news channel appears to show a  Border Patrol agent yelling at migrants  nearing the border to get back  he then fires rubber bullets and people  scatter  it was like something out of a movie  this migrant woman said everyone started  running  Mexico's interior ministry said around  500 migrants tried to violently enter  the u.s. Sunday US authorities claimed  some of the migrants threw rocks and  other projectiles at US Customs and  Border Patrol in recent weeks thousands  of mostly Central American migrants have  arrived in Tijuana by Caravan more than  5,000 migrants have been camped out in  and around a Sports Complex hoping to  soon seek asylum in the US but fewer  than a hundred asylum petitions are  being processed at the San Ysidro Port  of Entry a day after clashes near the  official port of entry yesterday it was  quickly closed in both directions by  border agents an unusual move at the end  of a busy holiday weekend people on foot  and in cars waited in long lines for  several hours late Sunday night the port  was reopened and as cars began filing in  people cheer Mexican authorities say  that they have arrested a 39 migrants  and they plan to deport anyone who tried  to enter the US illegally  the Mexican government also says that  since mid-october when the caravan first  started to arrive here they have  deported nearly 11,000 people Central  Americans back to their home countries  John Maria thank you
 

